WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.720
The House of Representatives will be voting later&nbsp;
this month on whether to make Washington, D.C.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.760
the fifty first state of the United States.&nbsp;
CBS News reporting that House Majority Leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.440
Steny Hoyer wrote in a letter to colleagues two,&nbsp;
three days ago that at some point during April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.240
the House will hold a vote. Do we make Washington,&nbsp;
D.C., the fifty first state? Now, of course. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.120
course. Of course. It will almost certainly pass&nbsp;
the House and it will almost certainly go nowhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.280
in the Senate. Most Republicans have expressed&nbsp;
that they are against making Washington, D.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.720
the fifty first state. This would not be and&nbsp;
will not be the first time that the House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.480
Representatives votes to do this. The House&nbsp;
already approved the D.C. statehood measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.680
in 2020 by a vote of 232 to 180, but it did&nbsp;
not even come to the floor of the Senate. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.240
in 2020, the Senate was controlled by Republicans.&nbsp;
Democrats now control the Senate. It's 50 50.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.720
Democrats have a majority because by controlling&nbsp;
the White House, Kamala Harris becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.920
the tiebreaking 50 first vote in the Senate. But&nbsp;
most legislation needs 60 votes to go forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.000
This is not the type of thing making Washington,&nbsp;
D.C. a fifty first state. That's not something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.440
you can move forward on via budget reconciliation&nbsp;
by every analysis that we've seen so far. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:32.960
need to find 10 Republicans, 10 Republicans. And&nbsp;
it is extraordinarily unlikely. We are not aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.000
of 10 Republicans that want to move forward&nbsp;
with Washington, D.C. as a fifty first state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.320
Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer has committed&nbsp;
to bringing it to the floor for a vote. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.000
this gets back to the filibuster. And when we&nbsp;
talked, what was it, January, maybe February, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.320
we talked about the possible paths forward on the&nbsp;
filibuster? One of the things that we explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:04.880
was even if there's something you could pass&nbsp;
with 50 votes, you still need 60 to end debate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.440
This is how this is how the filibuster works. And&nbsp;
we talked about some filibuster reforms, making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:17.120
a talking filibuster, requiring 40 requiring 40&nbsp;
senators in the Senate to keep debate going at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.440
all times. We've talked about all these things.&nbsp;
None of these changes have been made. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:28.080
we listed D.C. statehood and Puerto Rican&nbsp;
statehood as specific possible issues that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.920
will go nowhere unless some kind of filibuster&nbsp;
reform is done. Well, it's happening now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.600
Sometime this month, the House is going to vote on&nbsp;
making Washington, D.C., a 50 first state. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.200
likely to pass. It will then go to the Senate and&nbsp;
by every indication, there will not even be a vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.840
on it. Of course, I am in favor of D.C. statehood.&nbsp;
We've talked before about how the population of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.720
Washington, D.C. at 700000 is larger than the&nbsp;
population of the state of Wyoming, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.080
two senators. It's larger than the population&nbsp;
of the state of Vermont, which has two senators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.800
Those in Washington, D.C., have no voting&nbsp;
representation in Congress, but they do have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.840
just as a sort of factual note, Eleanor Holmes&nbsp;
Norton is a representative for Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:21.360
D.C. in the House, but is a non-voting&nbsp;
delegate. So that's the latest I've said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.160
I would love to see this issue of D.C. statehood&nbsp;
dealt with in a serious manner by Joe Biden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.480
The House is going to vote and then it is likely&nbsp;
going to stall in the Senate. And at a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.680
point, Democrats and Joe Biden are going to have&nbsp;
to decide, are we committed to getting these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.960
things done to the degree that we are going to&nbsp;
do something about the filibuster, understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.440
that any changes they do make to the filibuster&nbsp;
could be a double edged sword that comes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.640
against them when they are not in control. I&nbsp;
support filibuster reform. I support Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.760
D.C. as a fifty first state. I would love to&nbsp;
hear from you. How should Democrats handle this?

